Ultra suspend: hrdl's rail payload, adopted whole.

THIS PATCH MUST NEVER REACH A PRODUCTION IMAGE.  It is carried only by the
`linux-pinenote-ultra' kernel, which is used only by the
`pinenote-reader-ultra' flavor, which exists only to answer the wake
question on a supervised bench.  `pinenote/scripts/preflight/validate-ultra-
rails-quarantine.sh' fails the build if it appears anywhere else.

WHY, and why the whole thing rather than a subset.

The 2026-08-07 handshake proved bl31 honours LINUX_PM_STATE=5, and the
device then woke from nothing.  R11 (2026-08-08) instrumented it: with
firmware diagnostics on, a WORKING mem suspend and the NON-RETURNING ultra
suspend were shown to receive bit-for-bit identical configuration --
cfg 0x5ec, wakeup 0x10, and in both cases bl31 printing "Enable GPIO0
interrupt as wakeup source".  So the failure is not misconfiguration; it is
downstream of arming.

What R11 also measured, for the first time on this device: ultra draws
roughly 3 mA against deep's ~20 mA (upper bound 10.2 mA even attributing
nothing to the recovery boot).  That is the difference between ~7 days of
standby and ~35, so the wake question is worth solving rather than closing.

The remaining difference from the only configuration anyone has working is
these five lines.  hrdl's commit ee2c553f78 states the mode-5 override "is
needed for the system to resume" -- i.e. rails-off and state 5 are a matched
pair, and our 2026-08-07 attempt ran the half nobody runs.  Their
sleep-mode-config (0x5ec) and wakeup-config (0x10) are already identical to
ours; the rails ARE the entire delta.

WHAT THIS TURNS OFF, and the consequence to expect:

  vcca_1v8_pmu (LDO_REG1)  VCCIO4 -- Wi-Fi SDIO and BT signalling, sdmmc1
                           vqmmc.  Wi-Fi/BT die across suspend; sdmmc1 is
                           therefore moved to cap-power-off-card so the
                           driver re-initialises the card instead of
                           assuming it kept power.
  vdda_0v9_pmu (LDO_REG3)  the SoC's own PMU-domain analog supply.  It has
                           zero DT consumers, so nothing in the tree can
                           tell you what breaks; hrdl kills it, so we do.
  vcc_3v3_pmu  (LDO_REG6)  pmuio1/pmuio2 -- the GPIO0 pad bank carrying
                           EVERY external wake interrupt on this board:
                           rk817 INT (pwrkey, RTC alarm, charger), touch,
                           pen, Wacom, BT/Wi-Fi host-wake and the cover.

That last one is the counter-intuitive part and it is the point.  Killing
the pad bank should make SoC GPIO wake impossible -- yet hrdl's device
resumes, which means wake in a working ultra is PMIC-mediated, not a GPIO0
interrupt.  Our rails-ON attempt kept the pad bank alive and still could not
wake, which is exactly what a configuration nobody supports looks like.

The cyttsp5 hunk is hrdl's [HACK] (693fee19), adapted: vcc_3v3_pmu feeds the
touch controller's vdd, so it returns cold and its power-on handshake can
fail.  Reset and continue rather than losing touch for the session.  Their
startup call is commented out in the original and remains so here -- this is
a known-incomplete recovery, carried because the alternative is no touch at
all after resume.

Origin: https://git.sr.ht/~hrdl/linux branch v6.19_ultra_suspend, commits
ee2c553f78 (DTS) and 693fee19 (cyttsp5).  Regenerated against our patched
7.0.11 tree rather than cherry-picked, because our forward-port patch shifts
the DTS line numbers.

diff -uNr a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3566-pinenote.dtsi b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3566-pinenote.dtsi
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3566-pinenote.dtsi	2026-08-08 13:32:11.491717819 -0600
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3566-pinenote.dtsi	2026-08-08 13:32:11.502744235 -0600
@@ -449,7 +449,7 @@
 				regulator-max-microvolt = <1800000>;
 
 				regulator-state-mem {
-					regulator-on-in-suspend;
+					regulator-off-in-suspend;
 				};
 			};
 
@@ -471,7 +471,7 @@
 				regulator-max-microvolt = <900000>;
 
 				regulator-state-mem {
-					regulator-on-in-suspend;
+					regulator-off-in-suspend;
 				};
 			};
 
@@ -504,7 +504,7 @@
 				regulator-max-microvolt = <3300000>;
 
 				regulator-state-mem {
-					regulator-on-in-suspend;
+					regulator-off-in-suspend;
 				};
 			};
 
@@ -894,7 +894,7 @@
 	bus-width = <4>;
 	cap-sd-highspeed;
 	cap-sdio-irq;
-	keep-power-in-suspend;
+	cap-power-off-card;
 	mmc-pwrseq = <&sdio_pwrseq>;
 	non-removable;
 	pinctrl-0 = <&sdmmc1_bus4>, <&sdmmc1_clk>, <&sdmmc1_cmd>;
diff -uNr a/drivers/input/touchscreen/cyttsp5.c b/drivers/input/touchscreen/cyttsp5.c
--- a/drivers/input/touchscreen/cyttsp5.c	2026-08-08 13:32:11.491774847 -0600
+++ b/drivers/input/touchscreen/cyttsp5.c	2026-08-08 13:32:11.512433689 -0600
@@ -956,11 +956,30 @@
 static int __maybe_unused cyttsp5_resume(struct device *dev)
 {
 	struct cyttsp5 *ts = dev_get_drvdata(dev);
+	int error = 0;
 
-	if (!device_may_wakeup(dev))
-		cyttsp5_power_control(ts, true);
+	if (!device_may_wakeup(dev)) {
+		error = cyttsp5_power_control(ts, true);
 
-	return 0;
+		/*
+		 * Under ultra suspend vcc_3v3_pmu is off, which takes this
+		 * controller's vdd with it, so the chip comes back cold and
+		 * the power-on handshake can fail.  Reset it and carry on
+		 * rather than propagating the error and losing touch for the
+		 * rest of the session.  Adapted from hrdl's [HACK] commit
+		 * 693fee19 -- their startup call is left commented out there
+		 * too, so this is a known-incomplete recovery.
+		 */
+		if (error) {
+			gpiod_set_value_cansleep(ts->reset_gpio, 1);
+			msleep(10);
+			gpiod_set_value_cansleep(ts->reset_gpio, 0);
+			msleep(20);
+			error = 0;
+		}
+	}
+
+	return error;
 }
 
 static SIMPLE_DEV_PM_OPS(cyttsp5_pm, cyttsp5_suspend, cyttsp5_resume);
